 'Watch-only backups': "<h2>Watch-only backups</h2>\n<p><b>Settings → Backup and Restore → Watch-only wallets</b> saves a collection of supported public wallets from this Node. It is useful for moving addresses, labels and history to another Node. It deliberately excludes signing wallets and private keys.</p>\n<h3>Make a collection</h3>\n<ol><li>Start Core and finish any blockchain-copy operation.</li><li>Choose <b>Backup</b> and a destination folder. Encryption is selected by default to protect the public wallets and their history.</li><li>Choose <b>Create wallet backup</b>. The tool identifies supported watch-only wallets and asks Core to make a consistent native public-only export.</li><li>Keep the saved .json or .gpg file private. Check the number of skipped wallets. Keep a separate trusted record of each public descriptor ID and the backup file SHA-256.</li></ol>\n<p>A wallet that was closed may be loaded briefly for inspection and then put back in its previous state. The collection keeps public descriptors, receiving/change-address state, labels, address-book entries and transaction records. Descriptors are the wallet's address instructions. The collection cannot authorize spending and cannot replace your original offline signing wallet.</p>\n<p>The export is packaged, optionally encrypted, written and flushed to the destination. It is not reopened in a second Core process and is not read back from USB before the saved confirmation. This avoids the extra export verification step; Core's native public-only export is still used rather than copying an active wallet database.</p>\n<h3>Restore it</h3>\n<ol><li>Use the same Core version and Bitcoin network, and start Core on the target Node.</li><li>Choose <b>Restore</b> in the Watch-only wallets row. Select the collection file; supply its passphrase only if it is encrypted.</li><li>Choose <b>Review backup</b>, compare the backup file SHA-256 and each public descriptor ID with the record you kept separately. When they match, approve the review and choose <b>Restore wallets</b>.</li><li>If a name already exists, a new restored name is used rather than overwriting the existing wallet. Repeating this backup's restore does not duplicate wallets already restored successfully.</li><li>Compare addresses and labels in Core and let any needed scan finish.</li></ol>\n<p>A multi-wallet restore can succeed partly: wallets already completed remain if a later one fails. Repeating the restore can continue the work. Limits are 32 wallets, 32 MiB for one public export and 64 MiB combined, also limited by free private RAM. MiB is a storage-size unit; those limits keep the temporary in-memory work bounded.</p>\n<p>A pruned chain cannot provide old blocks it no longer has. Public exports also cannot recreate missing <b>hardened</b> address derivations that require private-key information: please keep the original Vault and review any address/scan warning.</p>\n<p>A single <b>watch-only.dat</b> exported straight from Vault is different from this collection. Open that file with <b>Bitcoin Core → File → Restore Wallet</b>. This menu expects Node's .json/.gpg collection format.</p>",
 'Core configuration': "<h2>Core configuration</h2>\n<p><b>Configuration</b> means settings, not your money or transaction history. Use <b>Settings → Backup and Restore → Bitcoin Core configuration</b> to transfer supported Core preferences without copying the operating system's network settings.</p>\n<h3>What is included?</h3>\n<p>Examples include the pruning limit; database cache, which is memory used to speed up data access; the number of verification threads, which are workers checking data; limits for pending transactions; maximum peer connections; and the upload target. Selected Core interface preferences include <b>Coin Control</b>, which lets you choose which available coins to spend, and the PSBT controls.</p>\n<p>Only recognized, validated options are copied. This is not an unchecked copy of every line in bitcoin.conf, Core's manual configuration file.</p>\n<h3>Save and restore</h3>\n<ol><li>Choose <b>Backup</b>, select a USB folder and choose whether to encrypt.</li><li>Choose <b>Save backup</b> and wait for the saved confirmation; there is no read-back verification pass.</li><li>On the target Node choose <b>Restore</b>, select the file and choose <b>Review backup</b>.</li><li>Read the settings, approve them and choose <b>Restore on next restart</b>.</li><li>Restart normally, then check Core's settings.</li></ol>\n<p>Wallets, wallet registrations, blocks, network credentials, source-computer data paths and commands/scripts are excluded. Unsupported options are not silently copied. A Node configuration backup already includes this portable Core subset, so a separate Core backup is optional when you also save Node configuration.</p>\n<p>Restoring a smaller pruning target can later remove older blocks. Restoring a setting does not bring back block files that are missing.</p>",
 'Node configuration': "<h2>Node configuration</h2>\n<p>This backup is a shortcut for setting up another Node the way you like it. Open <b>Settings → Backup and Restore → Node configuration</b>. A <b>snapshot</b> here means a saved selection of your current settings, not a complete copy of the computer's disk.</p>\n<h3>What it keeps</h3>\n<p>It can keep supported saved Wi-Fi networks and their stored passwords, Ethernet profiles, the managed WireGuard VPN and privacy choice, time zone and automatic-time choice, keyboard layout, display scale, mouse speed for matching device names and supported Core preferences. New backups also keep Keyboard and Mouse repeat/Num Lock choices and supported mouse/touchpad options. Restore these newer backups on a Node with this feature; older Node versions may reject the new format. This Node still reads earlier configuration backups.</p>\n<p>A network profile is the saved information needed to connect. Display scale controls how large interface elements look. The backup does not save an old clock reading to set the computer back to that time later.</p>\n<h3>Create the copy</h3>\n<ol><li>Finish applying the settings you want to keep and connect a USB drive.</li><li>Choose <b>Backup → Choose folder…</b> and select the drive or a folder on it.</li><li>Leave <b>Encrypt backup (optional)</b> selected and enter the same passphrase twice. To deliberately save a plain .json file, clear the checkbox.</li><li>Choose <b>Save backup</b> or <b>Save encrypted backup</b>. Wait for the saved confirmation and note the filename. There is no read-back verification pass.</li></ol>\n<p><b>A plain Node backup can expose saved Wi-Fi passwords and VPN private keys.</b> Keep it private. Encryption protects the saved file, not the running computer. Please do not store the only copy of the passphrase inside the file that needs that passphrase to open.</p>\n<h3>Apply it to another Node</h3>\n<ol><li>Open the same category and choose <b>Restore</b>.</li><li>Select the .json or .gpg file. Only an encrypted file needs its backup passphrase.</li><li>Choose <b>Review backup</b>. Check the time zone, networks, privacy profile and omissions. If the backup includes a managed VPN, compare the WireGuard peer public key, endpoint and DNS addresses with your trusted configuration before approving.</li><li>Tick <b>I trust this backup and approve the settings shown above</b>, then choose <b>Restore on next restart</b>.</li><li>Restart normally. The checked settings are applied before networking starts. <b>Check pending restore</b> shows the staged request or its result; <b>Cancel pending restore</b> cancels an unapplied request before restart.</li></ol>\n<h3>What does not move</h3>\n<p>The restore keeps local wallets and network profiles found only on the target computer. It does not clone login passwords/accounts, disk encryption, disk identifiers, Bluetooth pairing keys, arbitrary scripts, external certificates or unsupported VPN plug-ins. Pair Bluetooth devices again on a different adapter. Mouse settings apply only when the device name matches, and only to capabilities that device offers.</p>\n<p>Only passwords actually stored in supported profiles can be transferred. Some workplace authentication tools store credentials elsewhere; missing passwords are not invented. Unsupported values and omissions are reported for you to review.</p>\n<p>If the old and new computers will be online together, they may need different manually assigned IP addresses and different WireGuard peers. Reusing the same address or tunnel identity can cause a conflict.</p>\n<p><b>This contains no blockchain or wallet database.</b> Use those separate backup rows. You do not normally need a separate Core configuration backup because Node configuration already includes the supported Core settings.</p>",
-'Networking': '<h2>Networking</h2>\n<h3>Connect to Wi-Fi or a cable</h3>\n<p>Click the <b>Network Status</b> icon just to the left of Restart. Choose your Wi-Fi network, enter its password and click <b>Connect</b>. Saved networks can reconnect automatically. Ethernet is a wired connection: connect the cable and the system normally sets it up automatically.</p>\n<p><b>Advanced settings</b> is for hidden Wi-Fi names, workplace networks and manually assigned IP addresses. An IP address is the network address used to reach a computer. You usually do not need to set one manually on a home router. A connection to the router is not proof that the Internet is reachable.</p>\n<p>If you open Core without a connection, Node offers network setup first. <b>Use Core offline</b> still lets you inspect local data. Changing Wi-Fi does not remove your chosen network protection rules.</p>\n<h3>Choose a privacy connection deliberately</h3>\n<p>Open <b>Settings → Bitcoin Core Network &amp; Privacy</b>. These choices affect how information leaves the computer; they do not make an unsafe computer safe.</p>\n<table border="1" cellpadding="10"><tr><th>Choice</th><th>What it does</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Clearnet</td><td>Core connects directly to Bitcoin peers. Node runs the Tor service only when the Tor peer profile or private transaction broadcast needs it. Peers see your public IP address; your Internet provider may recognize Bitcoin traffic.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tor</td><td>Core\'s peer-to-peer traffic goes through the local Tor service rather than directly to peers. The operating system\'s other traffic, including software updates, still uses its ordinary connection.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>WireGuard VPN</td><td>The computer routes traffic through the configured encrypted VPN tunnel. Its protection rules block ordinary fallback outside that tunnel, apart from reaching the VPN server and essential connection setup. The VPN provider becomes someone you must trust.</td></tr></table>\n<p>A <b>firewall</b> is a set of rules allowing or blocking network traffic. The VPN\'s <b>kill switch</b> means these rules block fallback if the tunnel stops. <b>DNS</b> is the service that translates website names into network addresses. This Node\'s managed WireGuard setup protects against unintended traffic outside its tunnel, including ordinary DNS and IPv6 traffic. Neither Tor nor a VPN guarantees anonymity.</p>\n<p>Import a supported full-tunnel WireGuard configuration from your provider or own server. It must have a numeric server address and explicit DNS settings. Configurations containing scripts or unsupported split routes are refused. Tunnel private keys are stored in protected system files. A split route sends only some traffic through the VPN; this tool is designed for the supported whole-device configuration instead.</p>\n<p>Changing the privacy profile first stops a running Core, checks and replaces the network policy, then restarts it. If it was already stopped, it stays stopped. If the change fails, Core remains stopped rather than silently using a less-private route. I2P setup and Tor bridges are not exposed in this interface.</p>\n<h3>Sending a transaction privately is a separate option</h3>\n<p><b>Private transaction broadcast</b> concerns the initial sending of a transaction, not every connection Core makes. It uses Core 32.1\'s private-broadcast route. The underlying feature can use short-lived Tor/I2P connections; this Node exposes the Tor configuration. The submitted transaction is not initially added to the local mempool by this route.</p>\n<p>Turning on Tor for this purpose also makes Tor available for ordinary peer selection. A failed private route must not silently fall back to standard sending. A returned transaction ID is a reference number, not proof that peers received the transaction or that a block confirmed it. Please check transaction status afterward.</p>\n<h3>Connect a Bluetooth keyboard or mouse</h3>\n<p>Open <b>Settings → Bluetooth Devices</b> or press <b>Ctrl+Alt+B</b>. Switch on the adapter, put the device into pairing mode, then choose <b>Search</b>, pair and connect. For a keyboard, type the displayed pairing code on that keyboard and press Enter when asked. Mark a device you own as trusted to help it reconnect.</p>\n<p>Pairing means the two devices remember each other. The installed Node saves those pairing details. After restart, wake or switch on the device. Pairing done only in the installation session must be repeated after installation. A wired or built-in keyboard and mouse are useful during setup; Ctrl+Alt+B also works in the installer. Bluetooth file transfer and network sharing are not enabled by this Node setup.</p>\n<p>If no Bluetooth adapter appears, please check any airplane-mode switch and the computer\'s firmware settings. Common adapter firmware is included, but some devices need different firmware or a compatible USB adapter.</p>\n<h3>Keyboard and Mouse</h3>\n<p>Open <b>Settings → Keyboard and Mouse</b>. On the <b>Keyboard</b> tab, choose the keyboard model, layout/language and variant. A layout says which character each key types; a variant is an alternative arrangement. Leave the variant on Default unless you need another one. You can enable or disable held-key repetition, set the delay and repetition rate, and choose whether Num Lock starts on. Use the test area after <b>Apply and Save</b>; please do not type passwords there.</p>\n<p>The chosen layout applies to your current desktop and to the login screen after restart. Num Lock follows your saved choice on the login screen and when the desktop starts. It starts on by default. Key repeat applies on the desktop. They do not replace an older encrypted installation\'s disk-unlock keyboard configuration.</p>\n<p>On <b>Mouse and Touchpad</b>, select a connected device and adjust pointer speed. Depending on its capabilities, you can swap the main button for left-handed use, reverse scrolling with Natural scrolling, enable tapping, ignore touchpad movement while typing, emulate a middle click, and choose movement/scroll methods. Greyed-out options simply are not offered by that device. Adaptive movement speeds up with your hand; Flat uses a steady relationship. Settings are remembered for matching device names and reapplied after reconnect or restart. They do not change Core or reconnect Bluetooth by themselves.</p>',
+'Networking': '<h2>Networking</h2>\n<h3>Connect to Wi-Fi or a cable</h3>\n<p>Click the <b>Settings</b> icon just to the left of Restart, then choose <b>Network Connections</b>. Choose your Wi-Fi network, enter its password and click <b>Connect</b>. Saved networks can reconnect automatically. Ethernet is a wired connection: connect the cable and the system normally sets it up automatically.</p>\n<p><b>Advanced settings</b> is for hidden Wi-Fi names, workplace networks and manually assigned IP addresses. An IP address is the network address used to reach a computer. You usually do not need to set one manually on a home router. A connection to the router is not proof that the Internet is reachable.</p>\n<p>If you open Core without a connection, Node offers network setup first. <b>Use Core offline</b> still lets you inspect local data. Changing Wi-Fi does not remove your chosen network protection rules.</p>\n<h3>Choose a privacy connection deliberately</h3>\n<p>Open <b>Settings → Bitcoin Core Network &amp; Privacy</b>. These choices affect how information leaves the computer; they do not make an unsafe computer safe.</p>\n<table border="1" cellpadding="10"><tr><th>Choice</th><th>What it does</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Clearnet</td><td>Core connects directly to Bitcoin peers. Node runs the Tor service only when the Tor peer profile or private transaction broadcast needs it. Peers see your public IP address; your Internet provider may recognize Bitcoin traffic.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tor</td><td>Core\'s peer-to-peer traffic goes through the local Tor service rather than directly to peers. The operating system\'s other traffic, including software updates, still uses its ordinary connection.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>WireGuard VPN</td><td>The computer routes traffic through the configured encrypted VPN tunnel. Its protection rules block ordinary fallback outside that tunnel, apart from reaching the VPN server and essential connection setup. The VPN provider becomes someone you must trust.</td></tr></table>\n<p>A <b>firewall</b> is a set of rules allowing or blocking network traffic. The VPN\'s <b>kill switch</b> means these rules block fallback if the tunnel stops. <b>DNS</b> is the service that translates website names into network addresses. This Node\'s managed WireGuard setup protects against unintended traffic outside its tunnel, including ordinary DNS and IPv6 traffic. Neither Tor nor a VPN guarantees anonymity.</p>\n<p>Import a supported full-tunnel WireGuard configuration from your provider or own server. It must have a numeric server address and explicit DNS settings. Configurations containing scripts or unsupported split routes are refused. Tunnel private keys are stored in protected system files. A split route sends only some traffic through the VPN; this tool is designed for the supported whole-device configuration instead.</p>\n<p>Changing the privacy profile first stops a running Core, checks and replaces the network policy, then restarts it. If it was already stopped, it stays stopped. If the change fails, Core remains stopped rather than silently using a less-private route. I2P setup and Tor bridges are not exposed in this interface.</p>\n<h3>Sending a transaction privately is a separate option</h3>\n<p><b>Private transaction broadcast</b> concerns the initial sending of a transaction, not every connection Core makes. It uses Core 32.1\'s private-broadcast route. The underlying feature can use short-lived Tor/I2P connections; this Node exposes the Tor configuration. The submitted transaction is not initially added to the local mempool by this route.</p>\n<p>Turning on Tor for this purpose also makes Tor available for ordinary peer selection. A failed private route must not silently fall back to standard sending. A returned transaction ID is a reference number, not proof that peers received the transaction or that a block confirmed it. Please check transaction status afterward.</p>\n<h3>Connect a Bluetooth keyboard or mouse</h3>\n<p>Open <b>Settings → Bluetooth Devices</b> or press <b>Ctrl+Alt+B</b>. Switch on the adapter, put the device into pairing mode, then choose <b>Search</b>, pair and connect. For a keyboard, type the displayed pairing code on that keyboard and press Enter when asked. Mark a device you own as trusted to help it reconnect.</p>\n<p>Pairing means the two devices remember each other. The installed Node saves those pairing details. After restart, wake or switch on the device. Pairing done only in the installation session must be repeated after installation. A wired or built-in keyboard and mouse are useful during setup; Ctrl+Alt+B also works in the installer. Bluetooth file transfer and network sharing are not enabled by this Node setup.</p>\n<p>If no Bluetooth adapter appears, please check any airplane-mode switch and the computer\'s firmware settings. Common adapter firmware is included, but some devices need different firmware or a compatible USB adapter.</p>\n<h3>Keyboard and Mouse</h3>\n<p>Open <b>Settings → Keyboard and Mouse</b>. On the <b>Keyboard</b> tab, choose the keyboard model, layout/language and variant. A layout says which character each key types; a variant is an alternative arrangement. Leave the variant on Default unless you need another one. You can enable or disable held-key repetition, set the delay and repetition rate, and choose whether Num Lock starts on. Use the test area after <b>Apply and Save</b>; please do not type passwords there.</p>\n<p>The chosen layout applies to your current desktop and to the login screen after restart. Num Lock follows your saved choice on the login screen and when the desktop starts. It starts on by default. Key repeat applies on the desktop. They do not replace an older encrypted installation\'s disk-unlock keyboard configuration.</p>\n<p>On <b>Mouse and Touchpad</b>, select a connected device and adjust pointer speed. Depending on its capabilities, you can swap the main button for left-handed use, reverse scrolling with Natural scrolling, enable tapping, ignore touchpad movement while typing, emulate a middle click, and choose movement/scroll methods. Greyed-out options simply are not offered by that device. Adaptive movement speeds up with your hand; Flat uses a steady relationship. Settings are remembered for matching device names and reapplied after reconnect or restart. They do not change Core or reconnect Bluetooth by themselves.</p>\n<h3>Display Settings</h3>\n<p>Open the <b>Settings</b> gear to the left of Restart, then choose <b>Display Settings</b>. Select a connected screen, a supported resolution and refresh rate, orientation, and interface size. With multiple screens, use <b>Mirror displays</b> to show the same desktop or <b>Extend desktop</b> for more workspace. Choose the primary display and the relative placement of the others. Built-in-only and external-only modes are offered when the connected screens support them.</p>\n<p>Choose <b>Apply</b> and check the result. Select <b>Keep changes</b> within 15 seconds; otherwise the previous working layout is restored. Use <b>Revert</b> to return immediately. After changing interface size, reopen an application if its controls have not resized. Confirmed settings are saved on the installed Node and reused for matching connected displays. A changed or disconnected monitor is checked again before a saved layout is used.</p>\n<h3>Transfer device settings</h3>\n<p>At the bottom of Settings, choose <b>Export device settings…</b> and save a new JSON file on USB. This includes the keyboard layout, supported keyboard and pointer preferences, and a saved display profile when available. It excludes wallets, passwords, network settings and old clock timestamps. The device profile is separate from a Node configuration backup.</p>\n<p>Use <b>Import device settings…</b>, open the profile and review it. <b>Apply input settings</b> applies keyboard preferences and supported settings for matching pointing devices. <b>Review display settings</b> opens the saved display choices for review; it does not apply them automatically. Use Apply and Keep changes after checking the proposed layout. The same device-profile format works on Node and Vault; Vault keeps imported preferences only in its current RAM session.</p>',
 'Status & diagnostics': "<h2>Status &amp; diagnostics</h2>\n<p>Open <b>Settings → Bitcoin Core Status</b> for a live overview of this computer's Core. It reads information locally; it does not ask a public website to tell you what your node is doing.</p>\n<h3>What the cards mean</h3>\n<p><b>Version</b> identifies the Core program you are running. <b>Blocks</b> are the blocks Core has checked. <b>Headers</b> are the block summaries it knows about. <b>Peers</b> are other Bitcoin computers it is connected to. <b>Mempool</b> means the transactions your node knows about that are waiting to be included in a block. <b>Network</b> distinguishes real Bitcoin, called mainnet, from testing networks. The privacy profile tells you whether the Node uses direct connections, Tor or the configured VPN.</p>\n<p>The storage figure is space on the actual blockchain filesystem—the storage area on its disk—not necessarily every disk attached to the computer. Values refresh automatically. When Core is stopped or cannot be reached, the dashboard says so rather than displaying a made-up success.</p>\n<h3>Open details without crowding the dashboard</h3>\n<p><b>Show technical details</b> opens its own window. You can resize or minimize it while keeping the main dashboard tidy. The information is grouped by blockchain, network/software, pending transactions, peer connections and wallet scanning when available.</p>\n<p>Use <b>Find a field or value…</b> to look for a particular item. <b>Expand all</b> opens the groups; <b>Collapse all</b> closes them. <b>Refresh</b> takes a new snapshot—a copy of the current readings—so the list does not keep moving while you read. The <b>Raw JSON</b> tab shows the same information in a structured text format mainly useful for technical support.</p>\n<p><b>Copy snapshot</b> copies it to the clipboard, not to a server. Please review it before sharing: wallet names and other details can reveal private information. Closing this window does not stop Core.</p>\n<p>A connected Core can keep synchronizing even if a separate software-download server is unavailable. The Updates chapter explains how to distinguish those situations.</p>",
 'Encrypt & Decrypt Files': "<h2>Encrypt &amp; Decrypt Files</h2>\n<p><b>Encrypt</b> means protect a file with a passphrase. <b>Decrypt</b> means recover its contents with that passphrase. These tools use GnuPG, usually called GPG. They do not encrypt the whole installed operating system or turn an online Node into an offline signer.</p>\n<p>This separate general file-encryption tool retains its recovery checks. The copy-only change applies to the four categories in Backup and Restore, not to this utility or to authenticated decryption.</p>\n<h3>First make a proper wallet backup</h3>\n<p>If the file is a wallet, use <b>Bitcoin Core → File → Backup Wallet</b> to save a consistent database in Private Workspace. A database is a structured file Core works with. Copying its live file while Core is changing it can produce an unusable copy; the built-in Backup Wallet command avoids that problem.</p>\n<ol><li>Open <b>Encrypt Files</b> on Home and select the saved file.</li><li>Choose a new .gpg destination on USB or persistent storage.</li><li>Enter and repeat a passphrase. Extra independent layers are optional. Each layer is another encryption step with its own passphrase; all required passphrases must be available for recovery.</li><li>Wait for verification to finish, then keep separate copies and store their required passphrases separately. Originals are not overwritten.</li></ol>\n<p>A verified single Core SQLite wallet.dat is encrypted directly. Other files or folders may first be packed into a <b>TAR archive</b>, which keeps names and folders together but is not itself encryption. The output uses interoperable OpenPGP AES-256 CFB+MDC: AES protects the contents, and the integrity check helps detect damaged or altered encrypted data. The tool checks each layer and complete recovery before publishing the final file.</p>\n<h3>Open an encrypted file</h3>\n<ol><li>Choose <b>Decrypt Files</b> and select the .gpg file.</li><li>Enter the needed passphrases. For layered Node/Vault files, their order does not matter to the recovery tool. For a normal single-layer AES-256 GPG file made elsewhere, choose <b>Recover one ordinary GPG layer only</b>.</li><li>Wait for decryption and its integrity check. The recovered contents are placed in a new folder inside Private Workspace, not directly on the source USB.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Files opens Private Workspace automatically</b> after successful decryption, showing the recovery folder without another success confirmation. Wrong passphrases, failed checks and cancellation do not open the folder as if recovery succeeded. Recovered files remain in RAM until you deliberately save or restore them elsewhere. No partial recovered plaintext is published after a failed or cancelled operation.</p>\n<p>To restore a public wallet file, use <b>Bitcoin Core → File → Restore Wallet</b> and select the recovered database. Core creates a <b>persistent</b> wallet on its installed disk; the active restored wallet is no longer just a RAM file. Check addresses and labels, wait for validation/scanning and resolve pruned-history warnings. In the Node/Vault setup, keep signing-wallet recovery on offline Vault, not this online computer. Practise recovery periodically, including on another trusted machine.</p>\n<h3>Use the USB sidebar</h3>\n<p>The input selectors and encrypted-file destination selector show <b>Places</b> and <b>USB / External media</b>. Click a listed drive to open or mount it. <b>Refresh USB</b> checks again without formatting anything. A read-only drive can be an input, not an output. Open and unlock encrypted media in Files first, then refresh.</p>\n<p>Files shows <b>Core</b>, <b>Private Workspace</b> and <b>PSBT Transfer Folder</b> in its widened sidebar. Core's data view is read-only; use Core's own commands to change wallets. Saving encrypted output to USB is different from decryption: decrypted output always goes to RAM first. Use Files to copy a recovered file elsewhere only deliberately.</p>",
 'Persistent storage': '<h2>Persistent storage</h2>\n<p><b>Persistent</b> means “still there after restarting.” The installed Node keeps its blockchain, chainstate, wallets and ordinary settings on disk. Its two temporary working folders are different:</p>\n<table border="1" cellpadding="10"><tr><th>Place</th><th>After shutdown or restart</th></tr><tr><td>Core data and ordinary files saved on disk</td><td>Kept on disk.</td></tr><tr><td>Private Workspace</td><td>Cleared: recovered files and temporary work live in RAM.</td></tr><tr><td>PSBT Transfer Folder</td><td>Cleared: public wallet/PSBT transfer files live in RAM.</td></tr><tr><td>A file copied to USB</td><td>Kept on that USB, if saving finished successfully.</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Delete a file in Files</h3>\n<p>Right-click a file on a writable drive. <b>Quick Delete</b> removes it immediately after confirmation, without overwriting it or using Trash. Choose <b>Secure Delete</b> to overwrite its contents three times and once more with zeros before removal. Flash storage, SSDs, snapshots and backups can retain other copies. To remove a wallet from Core and its startup list, use <b>Wallets</b> on Home.</p>\n<h3>Three Places in Files</h3>\n<p>Files opens <b>Core</b>, a read-only view containing wallet folders, blocks, chainstate and logs. The left-hand Places list is Core, Private Workspace, PSBT Transfer Folder; connected drives appear under Devices. The default width is wide enough for names, and a wider manual choice is preserved.</p>\n<p>Private Workspace and PSBT Transfer Folder are separate shared RAM locations available to the desktop and Core. Closing a window does not clear them; restarting or losing power does. Ordinary suspend leaves RAM powered and is not a way to clear the workspace. The old disk-based Public Wallet Transfer folder is not automatically erased; any old files left there remain persistent.</p>\n<p>Before encryption/decryption, Node verifies that the private workspace really is on its dedicated RAM filesystem. If that check fails, it stops rather than silently writing plaintext to disk. Node disables swap and hibernation; swap is disk space an operating system might otherwise use to hold memory pages. The workspace is limited by available memory, so please keep temporary files small while Core is busy.</p>\n<p>Copying outside RAM makes that new copy persistent. Restoring a wallet into Core also makes a persistent copy. Encrypting a backup does not erase or protect its original file or the active wallet database.</p>\n<h3>USB access and safe removal</h3>\n<p>Node\'s backup and file tools, Core, KeePassXC, Editor and Files show removable media in their file sidebars. Node\'s own choosers refresh live and provide <b>Refresh USB</b>. If a USB is connected while Core\'s or KeePassXC\'s chooser is already open, reopen the chooser or select <b>USB Media</b>.</p>\n<p>Core can read and write through its checked USB view—for example a wallet backup or PSBT—without receiving write access to the rest of the operating system. Existing USB files keep their permissions; new Core exports belong to the desktop account on filesystems that support owners. A protected/read-only drive remains read-only. Unlock an encrypted drive in Files before using it in another program.</p>\n<p>For removal, finish saving and close files on that drive. Core\'s USB view can keep it busy while Core is open, so please <b>stop Core normally, then use Files → Eject/Unmount</b>. Eject asks the system to finish writes and release the drive. If another application still has a file open, close it and retry. Do not pull out the drive while copying. Reconnecting creates a fresh checked view, not a stale path to the old device.</p>\n<h3>End a session</h3>\n<p>If both RAM folders are empty, Restart and Shut Down show the ordinary confirmation. If either contains anything, including a hidden file or empty folder, two warnings are followed by a five-minute countdown. Cancel, Esc or closing that window keeps the session open. <b>Restart now</b> or <b>Shut down now</b> skips the remaining wait. Core stops carefully only when the countdown finishes or the immediate action is chosen.</p>\n<p>Please finish or cancel an open encryption job first and copy any RAM files you need. Shutdown warnings do not verify your backups. A sudden power loss also loses RAM contents. RAM-only storage limits leftovers on disk; it does not protect files from an already compromised running system or promise instant physical memory sanitization.</p>\n<h3>KeePassXC and time settings</h3>\n<p>KeePassXC opens its passphrase generator with six random EFF-list words as a starting choice. Choose a suitable length and generate each secret independently. You do not need a password database to use the generator. Its temporary profile/cache are in RAM; changes survive reopening during this session but reset after boot. KeePassXC clears copied secrets after 10 seconds by default. To change the timeout, open <b>Tools → Settings → Security</b>. Save a password database you need to keep on USB or persistent storage, not only in RAM.</p>\n<p>Click the clock or use <b>Settings → Date &amp; Time</b>. Choose your time zone, such as Europe/Zagreb, and automatic network time. Turn automatic time off to enter a date/time manually. <b>Apply and Save</b> preserves the settings after restart. Automatic time may wait for a reachable time server. In Tor peer mode, time synchronization uses ordinary operating-system networking; in WireGuard mode it follows the VPN policy. Manual time works offline.</p>',
